-- Update the widget-builder skill's data-adapters.md reference to include the pipeline dependency type.
-- Also add a brief note to the main SKILL.md about pipelines for dynamic data.

-- 1. Update data-adapters.md resource
UPDATE agent_skill_resources
SET content = E'# Data Adapters\n\nThere are three ways to feed data into a widget: internal adapters, integration calls, and pipelines.\n\n## Internal Adapters\n\nFor data that lives inside Vibey (campaigns, tasks, contacts, etc.). The frontend fetches it directly from the database.\n\n```json\n{ "type": "internal", "adapter": "tasks", "params": { "limit": 20 } }\n```\n\nSupported adapters: campaigns, tasks, contacts, analytics, agents, leads, missions, custom_objects, campaign_team, campaign_stripe_overview, campaign_detail, campaign_deliverables, campaign_offers, campaign_avatars.\n\n## Integration Calls\n\nFor a single API call to a connected service (YouTube, Stripe, Slack, Notion, etc.). Use the action slug exactly as returned by discovery.\n\n```json\n{ "type": "integration", "integration": "youtube", "action": "YOUTUBE_GET_CHANNEL_DETAILS", "params": { "part": ["snippet", "statistics"] } }\n```\n\nThe data lands under `/<ACTION_SLUG>` in the data model, so the widget binds to paths like `/YOUTUBE_GET_CHANNEL_DETAILS/items`.\n\n## Pipelines\n\nWhen the user wants a widget that refreshes with live data — like "top 5 videos by views" or "unanswered Slack messages" — a single API call is not enough. You need to chain steps: fetch a list, enrich with details, sort, and limit.\n\nPipelines run server-side every time the widget loads. The user always sees fresh data, with no hardcoded IDs or values baked in from the conversation.\n\nUse a pipeline whenever the user''s request involves ranking, filtering, sorting, or combining data across multiple API calls.\n\n```json\n{\n  "type": "pipeline",\n  "outputKey": "top_videos",\n  "steps": [\n    {\n      "type": "integration",\n      "integration": "youtube",\n      "action": "YOUTUBE_LIST_CHANNEL_VIDEOS",\n      "params": { "channelId": "me", "maxResults": 50 }\n    },\n    {\n      "type": "integration",\n      "integration": "youtube",\n      "action": "YOUTUBE_GET_VIDEO_DETAILS_BATCH",\n      "params": {\n        "id": { "$ref": "steps.0.items[*].id.videoId" },\n        "parts": ["snippet", "statistics"]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      "type": "transform",\n      "sort": { "field": "statistics.viewCount", "order": "desc", "numeric": true },\n      "limit": 5\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe widget definition then binds to `/top_videos/items` (the outputKey becomes the root path).\n\n### How steps work\n\nSteps run in order. Each step can be:\n\n- **integration** — calls a connected service (same format as standalone integration calls)\n- **internal** — fetches from a Vibey adapter\n- **transform** — sorts, limits, or filters the previous step''s output\n\n### Referencing previous steps with $ref\n\nLater steps can reference earlier step outputs using `{ "$ref": "steps.N.path" }` in their params.\n\n| Syntax | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| `steps.0` | Entire output of step 0 |\n| `steps.0.items` | The `items` field from step 0 |\n| `steps.0.items[*].id` | Pluck `id` from every element in `items` (produces an array) |\n| `steps.0.items[*].id.videoId` | Pluck nested `id.videoId` from every element |\n\nThe `[*]` wildcard maps over an array — it extracts the given field from each element and returns a flat array. This is how you pass a list of IDs from a listing call into a batch details call.\n\n### Transform operations\n\nTransform steps operate on the previous step''s output. If the output is an object with `items` or `data` arrays, the transform auto-detects and operates on that array.\n\n| Operation | Format | Example |\n|-----------|--------|---------|\n| sort | `{ "field": "path.to.value", "order": "asc"/"desc", "numeric": true/false }` | Sort by view count descending |\n| limit | number | Take first 5 items |\n| filter | `{ "field": "path", "op": "eq"/"gt"/"lt"/"contains"/"exists", "value": ... }` | Keep only items where status = "active" |\n\n### More pipeline examples\n\n**Top 10 Instagram posts by engagement:**\n```json\n{\n  "type": "pipeline",\n  "outputKey": "top_ig_posts",\n  "steps": [\n    {\n      "type": "integration",\n      "integration": "instagram",\n      "action": "INSTAGRAM_GET_IG_USER_MEDIA",\n      "params": { "limit": 50 }\n    },\n    {\n      "type": "transform",\n      "sort": { "field": "like_count", "order": "desc", "numeric": true },\n      "limit": 10\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Active tasks sorted by due date:**\n```json\n{\n  "type": "pipeline",\n  "outputKey": "active_tasks",\n  "steps": [\n    { "type": "internal", "adapter": "tasks", "params": { "limit": 100 } },\n    {\n      "type": "transform",\n      "filter": { "field": "status", "op": "eq", "value": "active" },\n      "sort": { "field": "due_date", "order": "asc" },\n      "limit": 20\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Choosing the right type\n\n- One-shot data from Vibey (tasks, campaigns, contacts) → **internal**\n- One-shot data from a connected service → **integration**\n- Ranked, filtered, or multi-step data from any source → **pipeline**\n\nWhen in doubt, prefer a pipeline — it produces a self-refreshing widget that always shows the latest data without hardcoded values from the conversation.',
    updated_at = now()
WHERE skill_key = 'widget-builder'
  AND file_path = 'references/data-adapters.md';

-- 2. Update the main SKILL.md to mention pipelines
UPDATE agent_skills
SET markdown_content = markdown_content || E'\n\n## Dynamic data with pipelines\n\nUsers often want widgets that refresh automatically — "top 5 videos by views", "most engaged Instagram posts this month", "unanswered Slack messages". These require chaining multiple API calls and sorting/filtering the results.\n\nInstead of hardcoding IDs or values you found during the conversation, use a **pipeline** dependency. Pipelines run server-side on every widget load, so the data is always fresh.\n\nSee `references/data-adapters.md` for the pipeline format, $ref syntax, transform operations, and worked examples.\n\nThe key idea: if the user says "show me my top X by Y", that is a pipeline — fetch the list, sort by Y, take top X. The widget definition binds to the pipeline''s outputKey path, and the data refreshes every time the page loads.',
    updated_at = now()
WHERE skill_key = 'widget-builder';
